    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Biliary atresia\n• Most common reason for pediatric liver\ntransplantation.\n• Best initial test: Ultrasound (triangular fibrotic cord at\nporta hepatis; no evidence of normal ductal anatomy;\nno gallbladder)\n• Most accurate test (next step): Percutaneous liver\nbiopsy (pathognomonic for this process)\n• Best initial treatment (palliative): Hepatic\nportojejunostomy (Kasai procedure)\n• Best long-term management: Liver transplant\nCrigler-Najjar syndrome, type I\n• Findings: jaundice, kernicterus (unconjugated bilirubin\ndeposition in brain), increase unconjugated bilirubin.\n• Absent UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. Presents early\nin life, but some patients may not have neurologic\nsigns until later in life.\nGilbert syndrome\n• Mild decrease in UDP-glucuronosyltransferase\nconjugation.\n• Asymptomatic or mild jaundice usually occurs with\nstress, illness, or fasting\nHemochromatosis\n• Mutation in HFE gene, located on chromosome 6.\n• Leads to abnormal (low) hepcidin production, and\nincrease intestinal iron absorption",
