    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Only option C is part of the important principles of weaning.\nIntroducing complementary foods (solid and liquid foods other\nthan breast milk or formula, also known as weaning foods) at\nthe right time during infancy is important for both nutrition and\nhealthy development.\nImportant Principles for Weaning\n• Begin weaning at 6 months of age.\n• At the appropriate age, encourage the use of a cup\ninstead of a bottle.\n• Introduce one new food at a time.\n• Energy density of foods should exceed that of breast\nmilk.\n• Provide iron-rich foods such as meats and iron-\nfortified cereals.\n• Encourage zinc intake with foods like meat, dairy\nproducts, wheat, and rice.\n• Keep phytate intake low to enhance mineral\nabsorption.\n• Continue breastfeeding until 12 months of age; then\nswitch to formula or cow’s milk.\n• Limit cow’s milk to no more than 24 oz per day.\n• Discourage fluids other than breast milk, formula, and\nwater.\n• Limit 100% fruit juice to 4–6 oz per day and avoid\nsugar-sweetened beverages.",
