    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 86,
    "stem": "Which of the following congenital heart\ndiseases has a “snowman appearance”\non chest x-ray?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "D-transposition of great\narteries"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Tetralogy of Fallot"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Total anomalous pulmonary\nvenous return"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Partial anomalous pulmonary\nvenous return"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Radiographic findings of congenital heart diseases\nTotal anomalous pulmonary venous return\n• Snowman appearance (large supracardiac shadow\ntogether with a normal cardiac shadow)\nD-transposition of great arteries\n• Egg-shaped heart (mild cardiomegaly, a narrow\nmediastinum). “Egg on a string” appearance on chest\nx-ray.\nTetralogy of Fallot\n• Boot-shaped heart (hypertrophied right ventricle\nresults in a raised and tilted rounded apical shadow,\nwhich is positioned higher than usual above the\ndiaphragm and pointing towards the left side of the\nchest)\nPartial anomalous pulmonary venous return\n• Scimitar syndrome - (anomalous vein draining into the\nIVC appears as a crescentic shadow along the right\nborder of the cardiac silhouette",
