    "id": "medqbank-pediatrics-0088",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 88,
    "stem": "Which of the following is the most\ncommonly diagnosed pituitary\nadenoma in children?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Prolactinoma"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Corticotropinoma"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Somatotropinoma"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Craniopharyngioma"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Primary hypersecretion of pituitary hormones by adenoma is\nrelatively uncommon in childhood.\nThe most common type is prolactinoma, followed by\ncorticotropinoma and somatotropinoma, which secrete\nprolactin, corticotropin, and growth hormone (GH),\nrespectively.\nAlthough thyrotropinoma is rarely reported in children,\nhypothalamic hamartomas that secrete excess gonadotropin-\nreleasing hormone can cause precocious puberty.",
