    "id": "notion-pediatrics-0001",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 1,
    "stem": "How can infant’s perception and cognition be demonstrated during the first months of life? Demonstrated by:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Crying when hungry or sleepy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Eye gazing, head turning and sucking"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Smiling when approached by mom or dad"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Waking up during feeding period"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "These behaviors are indicative of an infant's developing sensory and motor skills. Eye gazing demonstrates visual engagement and recognition, head turning signifies responsiveness to auditory stimuli, and sucking is a reflexive response linked to feeding and comfort.\nThis choice encompasses a range of behaviors that reflect the infant's developing sensory and motor skills, including visual engagement, responsiveness to auditory stimuli, and reflexive responses linked to feeding and comfort. It provides a comprehensive overview of the early signs of cognitive and perceptual development in infants.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Infants often communicate their basic needs, such as hunger or sleepiness, through crying. This behavior reflects their ability to perceive discomfort and respond to it.",
      "B": "These behaviors are indicative of an infant's developing sensory and motor skills. Eye gazing demonstrates visual engagement and recognition, head turning signifies responsiveness to auditory stimuli, and sucking is a reflexive response linked to feeding and comfort.\nThis choice encompasses a range of behaviors that reflect the infant's developing sensory and motor skills, including visual engagement, responsiveness to auditory stimuli, and reflexive responses linked to feeding and comfort. It provides a comprehensive overview of the early signs of cognitive and perceptual development in infants.",
      "C": "Social smiling typically emerges around 6 to 8 weeks of age, indicating a cognitive and emotional response to familiar faces. This behavior reflects the infant's ability to recognize and differentiate between caregivers.",
      "D": "The timing of waking during feeding periods suggests the establishment of circadian rhythms. This behavior reflects the infant's ability to anticipate and respond to regular feeding cues, demonstrating a developing sense of time and routine."
    },
