      "A": "This approach involves engaging with the parent while minimizing direct eye contact with the child. It may be less threatening and help to reduce anxiety, allowing for a more cooperative examination. However, it may also depend on the child's individual temperament and comfort level.",
      "B": "This strategy involves conducting the examination with the child seated on the parent's lap. This can provide a sense of security and comfort for the child, potentially making them more cooperative during the examination. It utilizes the familiar presence of the parent to ease any apprehension.",
      "C": "Directly engaging with the child through eye contact and conversation can help build rapport and establish a connection. However, for some infants, direct eye contact may be perceived as intimidating, leading to resistance during the examination.",
      "D": "Offering a small, washable toy can serve as a distraction and a source of comfort for the child. It provides a positive association with the examination and may help keep the child occupied, making the process smoother."
