    "id": "notion-pediatrics-0078",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 78,
    "stem": "What imaging modality shows either acute pyelonephritis or renal scarring when positive?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Abdominal and Pelvic MRI"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Dimercaptosuccinic acid (DMSA) Renal Scan"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Kidney and Urinary Bladder Ultrasound"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Voiding Cystourethrogram (VCUG)"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "DMSA Renal Scan is a nuclear medicine imaging modality that is particularly useful for assessing both acute pyelonephritis and renal scarring. In cases of acute pyelonephritis, DMSA can detect areas of the kidneys that are inflamed or infected. Additionally, it is commonly used to identify renal scarring, a potential consequence of recurrent or severe urinary tract infections, especially in children. The scan involves the injection of a radioactive material that is taken up by functioning renal tissue, allowing visualization of the kidneys' structure and function.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Abdominal and Pelvic MRI can provide detailed images of the kidneys and surrounding structures. While it is not the primary modality for diagnosing acute pyelonephritis, it may be used in certain cases where MRI is indicated. It can also be helpful in identifying anatomical abnormalities.",
      "B": "DMSA Renal Scan is a nuclear medicine imaging modality that is particularly useful for assessing both acute pyelonephritis and renal scarring. In cases of acute pyelonephritis, DMSA can detect areas of the kidneys that are inflamed or infected. Additionally, it is commonly used to identify renal scarring, a potential consequence of recurrent or severe urinary tract infections, especially in children. The scan involves the injection of a radioactive material that is taken up by functioning renal tissue, allowing visualization of the kidneys' structure and function.",
      "C": "Ultrasound is a valuable imaging tool for detecting structural abnormalities, but it may not be as sensitive as DMSA in identifying acute pyelonephritis. It is often used as the initial imaging modality and can reveal features such as hydronephrosis. However, it may not be as specific for detecting renal scarring compared to DMSA.",
      "D": "VCUG primarily evaluates the anatomy of the bladder and urethra and is not a direct imaging modality for acute pyelonephritis or renal scarring. It is typically performed to assess vesicoureteral reflux (VUR)."
    },
