    "id": "notion-pediatrics-0080",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 80,
    "stem": "Situation 15 – Amelita, 16 years old, came to the clinic for delayed menarche. She had no other subjective complaints. The rest of her history was unremarkable. Her breast and pubic hair development were both Tanner stage 4. The rest of her physical examination findings were normal. What is TRUE regarding the definition Primary Amenorrhea? No menstrual flow .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "For more than 3 months"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "By age of 14 years"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "By age of 16 years"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "For more than 1 month"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Primary amenorrhea is defined as the failure of initiation of menses by age 14 in the absence of secondary sexual characteristics or the absence of menarche by age 16 regardless of the presence of normal growth and development of secondary sexual characteristics.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "This statement does not align with the traditional definition of primary amenorrhea. Typically, primary amenorrhea is assessed based on the absence of menstrual flow by a specific age rather than a specific duration.",
      "B": "Primary amenorrhea is defined as the failure of initiation of menses by age 14 in the absence of secondary sexual characteristics or the absence of menarche by age 16 regardless of the presence of normal growth and development of secondary sexual characteristics.",
      "C": "This is another recognized threshold for defining primary amenorrhea. If a girl has not experienced menstrual flow by the age of 16, it is considered a reason for investigation.",
      "D": "This option does not align with the traditional definition of primary amenorrhea. Primary amenorrhea is not typically diagnosed based on the absence of menstruation for just one month."
    },
