    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0021",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 21,
    "stem": "Which one of the following forms of juvenile\nidiopathic arthritis is most likely to be associated\nwith serious eye complications?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Polyarticular arthritis that is seropositive for\nrheumatoid factor"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Polyarticular arthritis that is seronegative for\nrheumatoid factor"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Oligoarticular arthritis without axial spine\ninvolvement"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Oligoarticular arthritis with axial spine\ninvolvement"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Systemic-onset juvenile rheumatoid arthritis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Patients with oligoarticular arthritis without axial\nspine involvement are most likely to develop chronic\nand potentially severe anterior uveitis, which can be\nclinically quite subtle even as it leads to progressive\nvisual loss. Up to 25% of patients in this subset may\ndevelop anterior uveitis, and the group that is ANA\npositive appears to be at highest risk. Patients with\naxial spine involvement can also develop anterior\nuveitis, but this tends to be acute, self-limited, and\neasily treatable.",
