    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0031",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 31,
    "stem": "A 3-year-old girl presents with generalized edema\nshortly after recovery from an upper respiratory\ninfection. Laboratory studies reveal marked\nalbuminuria, as well as hypoalbuminemia and\nhyperlipidemia. Prior similar episodes responded\nto steroids. The most likely diagnosis is",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "FSGS"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "membranous GN"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "lipoid nephrosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "PSGN"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "RPGN"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "SIMILAR TO PREVIOUS BOARD EXAM\nCONCEPT/PRINCIPLE. Our Pediatrics exam featured\nnumerous renal pathology questions as the stem case.\nIt is therefore essential to nail the diagnosis at the\nstart to keep you on track for the next questions. The\ncombination of generalized edema, massive\nproteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, and hyperlipidemia\nconstitutes the nephrotic syndrome, the prototype of\nwhich is minimal change disease (lipoid nephrosis).\nThis disorder characteristically occurs in young\nchildren and demonstrates intracytoplasmic lipid in\nthe proximal convoluted tubules, a paucity of\nglomerular abnormalities by light microscopy, and\n“fusing” (absence) of the epithelial foot processes by\nelectron microscopy.",
