    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0033",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 33,
    "stem": "A 3-year-old boy, an inner city resident, has\nmultiple bony abnormalities, including bowlegs and\nknock-knees, thickening of the skull with frontal\nbossing, knobby deformities of the costochondral\njunctions and, at the ends of the long bones,\ndistortion of the rib cage with flaring over the\ndiaphragm, and pigeon-breast deformity. A\ndecrease in which of the following is characteristic\nof this condition?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Bony osteoblastic activity"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Calcification of osteoid"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Release of parathyroid hormone"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Serum alkaline phosphatase activity"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Synthesis of osteoid"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The child has rickets, which is caused by failure of\naction of calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol), the\nactive form of vitamin D. The bony abnormalities in\nrickets are caused by failure of osteoid matrix to\ncalcify, leading to excess accumulation of osteoid,\nincreased thickness of the epiphyseal growth plates,\nand other skeletal deformities. Many of the effects of\ncalcitriol deficiency are mediated by increased release\nof parathyroid hormone (PTH). PTH stimulates bony\nosteoblastic activity, which is mirrored by an increase\nin serum alkaline phosphatase.",
