    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0035",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 35,
    "stem": "A 2-year-old boy presents with his third bone\nfracture within the past several months. There is no\nhistory or evidence of trauma. Several close family\nmembers have been similarly affected. The child is\nsmall for his age, and the sclerae are tinged a bluish\ncolor. Radiographs reveal generalized osteopenia\nand evidence of multiple fractures, both old and\nnew. Which of the following is the usual mode of\ninheritance of this disorder?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Autosomal dominant"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Autosomal recessive"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "X-linked dominant"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "X-linked recessive"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Mitochondrial"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Osteogenesis imperfecta (or brittle bone disease) is an\nautosomal dominant disorder characterized by\nmultiple fractures with minimal trauma. It is caused\nby mutations in either of the genes that code for type I\ncollagen and is manifested by connective tissue\nabnormalities affecting the bones, teeth, skin, and\neyes. The sclerae can appear blue due to translucency\nof the thin connective tissue overlying the choroid.\nThe disorder occurs in several variants defined by the\nage of onset and the severity of the fractures. In the\nless obvious cases, the principal differential diagnostic\nconsideration is child abuse.",
