    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0174",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 174,
    "stem": "The following factors indicate worse prognosis in a\npatient with Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia\nEXCEPT:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Age under 2"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Presentation in adolescence"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Peripheral blood blast greater than 100,000"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Presence of Philadelphia chromosome or\nt(9;22)"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "translocation of chromosome 12 and 21 or\nt(12;21)"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "E",
    "explanation": "t(12:21) usually indicates better prognosis. the\npresence of t(9:22) in ALL means that it has also a\nCML property which makes the case worse. Robbins\n8th ed page 603. Factors associated with worse\nprognosis are (1) age under 2; (2) presentation in\nadolescence or adulthood; (3) peripheral blood blast\ncounts greater than 100000; (4) the presence of\nPhiladelphia chromosome. Favorable prognostic\nmarkers include (1) an age of 2 to 10; (2) a low white\ncell count; (3) hyperploidy; (4) trisomy of\nchromosomes 4, 7, and 10, and; (5) the presence of a\nt(12:21)",
