    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0281",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 281,
    "stem": "What is the easiest way to confirm the diagnosis of\nbilary atresia in a 1 month old infant presenting\nwith cholestatic jaundice?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Presence of a triangular cord sign in the\nultrasound of the liver"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Intact lobular structure on liver biopsy"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Poor hepatic uptake of radioactive material but\nwith slow excretion into the gut"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Firm consistency of the liver on palpation"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Elevated levels of anti-mitochondrial antibodies"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Abdominal ultrasound is a helpful diagnostic tool in\nthe evaluation of neonatal cholestasis because it will\nidentify choledocholithiasis, perforation of the bile\nduct, or other structural abnormalities of the biliary\ntree such as a choledochal cyst. In patients with biliary\natresia, ultrasound may detect associated anomalies\nsuch as abdominal polysplenia and vascular\nmalformations. Ultrasonographic triangular cord (TC)\nsign, which represents a cone-shaped fibrotic mass\ncranial to the bifurcation of the portal vein, may be\nseen in patients with biliary atresia",
