    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0296",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 296,
    "stem": "A 10 year old boy presented with a 4 day history of\nfever, sore throat, dysphagia and odynophagia. On\nPE his VS are BP 100/60, PR 118, RR 21, T 38.6.\nThere was a noted asymmetric tonsillar bulge with\ndisplacement of the uvula. The rest of the\nexamination was normal. What is the most likely\ndiagnosis for this case?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Peritonsillar abscess"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Laryngeotracheobronchitis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Retropharyngeal abscess"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Diphtheria"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Epiglottitis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Peritonsillar cellulitis/abscess, which is relatively\ncommon compared to the deep neck infections, is\ncaused by bacterial invasion through the capsule of\nthe tonsil, leading to cellulitis and/or abscess\nformation in the surrounding tissues. Clinical\nmanifestations include sore throat, fever, trismus, and\ndysphagia. Physical examination reveals an\nasymmetric tonsillar bulge with displacement of the\nuvula. An asymmetric tonsillar bulge is diagnostic, but\nit may be poorly visualized because of trismus",
