    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0354",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 354,
    "stem": "A 3 year old girl has a 2 week history of fever\nassociated with headache, lethargy and vomiting.\nShe has a history of perioral cyanosis and dyspnea\nwith exertion ever since she was an infant. She\nsuddenly has a 10 minute tonic-clonic seizure. The\nchild is obtunded afterwards, with a temperature of\n38.2C, PR 118/min, BP of 100/70. Perioral cyanosis\nis noted at rest. A harsh pan systolic murmur is\nheard along the left sternal border. Fundoscopy\nreveals papilledema. She is also noted to have right\nsided weakness. The most likely cause of her\nhemiparesis is:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "A brain tumor"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Neurocysticercosis"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Meningitis"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "A brain abscess"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Based on the patient’s history, she most likely has\nTetralogy of Fallot, which predisposes her to having a\nbrain abscess. Other findings supporting this aetiology\nis the presence of increased ICP (papilledema) and a\nfocal neurologic deficit, which makes meningitis less\nlikely.",
