    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0488",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 488,
    "stem": "Joy, brought her 3-year old son in the middle of the\nnight, who has developed noisy breathing on\ninspiration, chest retractions, and a barking cough.\nHe has a mild upper respiratory tract infection for 2\ndays. Which would be the most helpful\nintervention?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "inhaled beta-agonist"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "ceftriaxone"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "racemic epinephrine"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "amoxicillin"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Laryngotracheobronchitis or viral croup presents with\ninspiratory stridor (extrathoracic symptom) as in this\ncase. Agents causing croup are usually parainfluenza\ntypes 1 and 3. Racemic epinephrine can be used to\nalleviate respiratory symptoms. Epiglottitis presents\nwith a more toxic-looking child and more acute\npresentation. The causative agent is H. influenzae,\nmanaged usually with ceftriaxone.",
