    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0587",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 587,
    "stem": "A 14-year old female was brought to the clinic due\nto pallor. This was associated with easy fatigability\nand frequent consumption of ice cubes. She had\nregular menses for 1 year. On physical examination,\nBP 100/60, PR 120, RR 22, temp 36.8, weight=40\nkg, height=160 cm. There was pale palpebral\nconjunctivae and palmar pallor. CBC showed Hgb\n90, Hct .28, WBC 5, platelet 250, 000. Serum ferritin\nand serum iron were low and total iron binding\ncapacity was low. If you plan to start the patient on\noral iron therapy, what is the recommended dose?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "500 mg of elemental iron once a day"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "2-3 mg/kg of elemental iron in 3 divided doses\ndaily"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "250 mg of elemental iron twice daily"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "4–6 mg/kg of elemental iron in 3 divided doses\ndaily"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "1 g of elemental iron once a week"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "In iron deficiency anemia, the therapeutic dose should\nbe calculated in terms of elemental iron; ferrous\nsulfate is 20% elemental iron by weight. A daily total\ndose of 4–6 mg/kg of elemental iron in 3 divided\ndoses provides an optimal amount of iron for the\nstimulated bone marrow to use.",
