    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0588",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 588,
    "stem": "A 3 year old male was brought to your clinic due to\nright elbow swelling. Few hours prior to consult,\npatient fell and hit his right elbow. There was noted\nrapid swelling of the affected joint and violaceous\ndiscoloration of the overlying skin. On further\nprobing, the mother claimed that the patient was\nnoted to have easy bruisability. On physical\nexamination, vital signs were normal. There was\nnoted tenderness and swelling on the right elbow\nwith limitation in the range of motion. If you are\nsuspecting Hemophilia, what initial screening test\nwill you request for the patient?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Prothrombin time"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Activated partial thromboplastin time"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Bleeding time"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Thrombin time"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Clotting time"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "The laboratory screening test that is affected by a\nreduced level of factor VIII or factor IX is PTT. Results\nof the other screening tests of the hemostatic\nmechanism (platelet count, bleeding time,\nprothrombin time, and thrombin time) are normal.\nThe specific assay for factors VIII and IX will confirm\nthe diagnosis of hemophilia.",
