    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0590",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 590,
    "stem": "A 12 year-old female was brought to your clinic\nroom due to blurring of vision. Patient had a one\nmonth history of blurring of vision of both visual\nfields described as being cloudy accompanied by\nheadaches. Patient was given paracetamol which\nafforded relief of the headache. On fundoscopy,\nthere was noted papilledema. MRI was requested\nwhich showed a solid tumor with cystic structures\ncontaining fluid of intermediate density in the\nsuprasellar area. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Intracranial aneurysm"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Pineoblastoma"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Mennigioma"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Chiasmal glioma"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Craniopharygioma"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "E",
    "explanation": "Craniopharyngioma (WHO grade I) is a common\ntumor of childhood, accounting for 7–10% of all\nchildhood tumors. These tumors are solid with cystic\ncomponents and occur within the suprasellar region.\nThey are minimally invasive, adhere to adjacent brain\nparenchyma, and engulf normal brain structures. MRI\ndemonstrates the solid tumor with cystic structures\ncontaining fluid of intermediate density. CT may show\ncalcifications associated with the solid and cystic wall\ncomponents. There is significant morbidity\n(panhypopituitarism, growth failure, visual loss)\nassociated with these tumors and their therapy owing\nto the anatomic location.",
