    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0592",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 592,
    "stem": "A 1 year old female was brought to the emergency\nroom due to 1 week history of fever. On physical\nexamination, PR 145, RR 32, temp 38.2. There was\ngeneralized maculopapular rash, erythema of the\noral and pharyngeal mucosa, the tongue was\nswollen, lips were dry and cracked, and cervical\nlymphadenopathy. This disease entity is of clinical\nsignificance due to its predilection to?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Renal arteries"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Aorta"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Coronary arteries"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cerebral arteries"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Pulmonary arteries"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Kawasaki disease (KD), formerly known as\nmucocutaneous lymph node syndrome and infantile\npolyarteritis nodosa, is an acute febrile vasculitis of\nchildhood. Fever is characteristically high (104°F or\nhigher), remittent, and unresponsive to antibiotics.\nThe duration of fever without treatment is generally\n1–2 wk, but it may persist for 3–4 wk. Prolonged fever\nis prognostic for the development of coronary artery\ndisease. In addition to fever, the five characteristic\nfeatures of Kawasaki disease are: bilateral bulbar\nconjunctival injection, usually without exudate;\nerythema of the oral and pharyngeal mucosa with\nstrawberry tongue and dry, cracked lips, and without\nulceration; edema and erythema of the hands and feet;\nrash of various forms (maculopapular, erythema\nmultiforme, or scarlatiniform) with accentuation in\nthe groin area; and nonsuppurative cervical\nlymphadenopathy, usually unilateral, with node size of\n≥1.5 cm. Perineal desquamation is common in the\nacute phase. Periungual desquamation of the fingers\nand toes begins 1–3 wk after the onset of illness and\nmay progress to involve the entire hand and foot.\nCardiac involvement is the most important\nmanifestation of Kawasaki disease. Myocarditis,\nmanifested as tachycardia out of proportion to fever\noccurs in at least 50% of patients.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "filename": "648945218-TopNotch-Merged.pdf",
        "pages": [
          986
        ],
        "answerPages": [
          1002
        ]
      },
      {
        "filename": "402059824-11-Topnotch-Pediatrics-SuperExam-pdf.pdf",
        "pages": [
          81
        ],
        "answerPages": [
          97
        ]
      }
    ],
    "status": "validated",
