    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0593",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 593,
    "stem": "A 2 year old was admitted at the pediatric ICU due\nto burn injuries to the trunk and both lower\nextremities. On the 5th day at the ICU, patient was\nnoted to have pink macules which later progressed\nto hemorrhagic nodules and eventually to ulcers\nwith ecchymotic and gangrenous centers with\neschar formation, surrounded by an intense red\nareola. If you are entertaining an infection caused\nby pseudomonas aeruginosa, what antibiotic has\ncoverage for this organism?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Ciprofloxacin"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Ceftriaxone"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Cloxacillin"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Doxycycline"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "The surfaces of burns or wounds are frequently\npopulated by Pseudomonas and other gram-negative\norganisms. The characteristic skin lesions of\nPseudomonas, ecthyma gangrenosum, whether\ncaused by direct inoculation or metastatic secondary\nto septicemia, begin as pink macules and progress to\nhemorrhagic nodules and eventually to ulcers with\necchymotic and gangrenous centers with eschar\nformation, surrounded by an intense red areola.\nAntibiotics with Pseudomonas coverage are\nCeftazidime, Ciprofloxacin, Tobramycin, Gentamicin,\nAztreonam, and Amikacin.",
