    "id": "superexam-pediatrics-0627",
    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 627,
    "stem": "A 5 week old infant has been brought to the clinic\ndue to jaundice. He was born full term, weighing 8\npounds and 9 ounces and he had an uncomplicated\ndelivery and neonatal course. Today, his weight is 9\npounds, 1 ounce, and his vital signs are normal. On\nexamination, his liver is enlarged and 4 cm below\nthe right costal margin. jaundice is present.\nLaboratory evaluation reveals a total bilirubin of\n12.9 mg/dL with a direct component of 5.9 mg/dL.\nWhich of the following is the next most appropriate\nnext step?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "begin phototherapy to treat jaundice"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "refer the patient for a liver transplant"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "begin ursodeoxhycholic acid to enhance bile\nflow"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "order an urgent abdominal ultrasound and\nradionucleotide imaging study of the liver"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "reassure the parents that no treatment is\nrequired as he likely has neonatal hepatitis"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "this patient has cholestasis, or retention of bile in the\nliver, manifested by elevated conjugated (direct\nbilirubin).in addition, the findings of hepatomegaly\nand poor growth support the diagnosis. MC causes of\ncholestatsis include biliary atresia and choledochal\ncyst.",
