    "subject": "pediatrics",
    "originalNumber": 695,
    "stem": "A 3-day old infant with a single 2nd heart sound has\nhad progressively deepening cyanosis since birth\nbut no respiratory distress. Chest radiography\ndemonstrates no cardiomegaly and normal\npulmonary vasculature. ECG shows an axis of 1200\nand RV prominence. Which of the following is most\nlikely responsible for the cyanosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Tetralogy of Fallot"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Transposition of the great vessels"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Tricuspid atresia"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "TAPVR"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Answer: B\nTransposition of the great vessels w/ an intact\nventricular septum presents w/ early cyanosis, a\nnormal-sized heart (classic egg on string in xray).\nNormal or slightly inc.pulmo.vascular markings and r\naxis deviation w/ RVH in ECG.",
